  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "A pear is projected vertically down from a height of $1.5\\,\\text{m}.$ It takes $0.5\\,\\text{s}$ to hit the ground. What was the initial velocity of the pear? **Do not round your answer**.\n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*\n\nThe initial velocity was{{field-1}}$\\text{m/s}.$",
  "worked_solution": "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The pear falls for $t=0.5\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n- The pear covers the distance of $s=1.5\\,\\text{m}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $u,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\ (1.5) & =u(0.5)+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8)(0.5)^{2} \\\\ 1.5 & =0.5u+1.225 \\\\ 0.275 & =0.5u \\\\ u & =0.55\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the initial velocity of the pear was $0.55\\,\\text{m/s}.$",
